te  what's called a directional derivative  so i'll just write that out here so the  directional derivative  um  and essentially the directional  derivative is literally the derivative  the derivative of f my scalar function  f in a direction  v so this is kind of cool we're used to  being able to take  the derivative of our function in the x  direction that's just partial partial x  we're used to being able to take it in  the y direction that's partial partial y  but what if i say okay take it in this v  direction i've got some random vector  v how do i take the derivative of f the  rate of change of  f in that direction is f getting bigger  smaller  by how much and mathematically we say  that this is the directional derivative  of f in the v direction this is how we  write it it's the derivative of  f in the v direction and it's super  simple it doesn't get simpler than this  it's just v  dotted with the gradient of f  remember the gradient of f is a vector  and i can dot it into this direction  v so i literally just see like where is  that where is  you know grad t grad f pointing and i  dot it with v  and that tells me how fast and if  they're perpendicular  then the directional derivative is zero  actually i kind of drew it so that the  gradient is almost perpendicular to this  v  in which case the directional derivative  would be zero  which means if i take a small step in  that direction my temperature doesn't  change  there are directions like that and you  have to normalize by  the length of this v because someone  might give you like a v  that's 10 units long or 0.10 units long  and it shouldn't  matter what the length of v is so the  directional derivative also super  important  okay one more example i want to really  like drive home how much i love the  gradient  how useful it is and i feel like when  i'm learning  math i like to see it a lot of different  ways because maybe one of them clicks  so the gradient allows me to compute the  rate  of change of